  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nToday through tomorrow night...\n\nThe latest GOES Night Fog at 4:30 AM reveals that most of the \ncoastal areas and inland stretching up to 15 miles in some areas \nwithin San Diego and Orange counties have been inundated with low\nclouds and overcast skies. These clouds may make it into the \nsouthern portion of the IE, namely the Temecula/Murrieta area, \notherwise these low clouds will likely remain in place through \nsunrise, then given how shallow the inversion is with the latest \nNKX 12z sounding, alluding to a shallow marine layer, also gives \nconfidence to a relatively quick mixing and scatter out of these \nclouds within the first couple of hours after sunrise occurs.\n\nThe ridge is beginning to shift a little further east today as \nthere will be further influence by an area of troughing upstream, \nwhich is going to continue to usher in relatively drier air into \nthe mid levels, and likely limit the chance of any thunderstorm \ndevelopment this afternoon as the mid-level moisture becomes more \nsuppressed and confined over Arizona. This troughing will also \nallow for a very gradual cooling to take place today through \ntomorrow, although highs will still be running around 5 to 10 \ndegrees above average for most locations. The marine layer will \nlikely struggle again to develop tonight, and remain mostly in \nplace along the coastal areas of San Diego County, then slowly \nextend further into some of the inland areas closer to daybreak.\n\nWednesday through the upcoming weekend...\n\nSlight cooling will continue through Wednesday, then the troughing \nupstream will begin to weaken and allow for the upper level ridge to \nfurther strengthen and expand back towards the west. This will allow \nfor temperatures to increase again well back above the seasonal \naverage for this time of year, by as much as 15 degrees for some \nlocations, especially within the Inland Empire. By Saturday, there \ncould be several locations within the IE topping out around 110 and \nabove, and likely warranting the issuance of more heat products at \nthat time. If planning to be outdoors for this upcoming weekend, it \nwould be wise to prepare for this heat by ensuring that there are \nplenty of drinking fluids, as the combination of the increase \nheat could put those sensitive to heat as risk of dehydration. \nThere will also be a better push of monsoonal waves transitioning\naround the plateau high located around the Four Corners region by\nlater this week, which will allow for influxes of mid-level \nmoisture to move in over the region and possibly allow for a few \nshowers and afternoon development of thunderstorms over the \nmountains and deserts, yet this will all be decided on the timing \nof these pulses of moisture as they transition over our area. \nOtherwise, hot and dry conditions will continue to be the case \ninto the upcoming weekend.",
